# 6️⃣ Recursive Binary Search

### C++ Code

```cpp
int binarySearch(int arr[], int low, int high, int target)
{
    if(low > high)
        return -1;

    int mid = low + (high-low)/2;

    if(arr[mid] == target)
        return mid;

    if(arr[mid] < target)
        return binarySearch(arr, mid+1, high, target);

    return binarySearch(arr, low, mid-1, target);
}
```

---

# 7️⃣ Why use

```cpp
mid = low + (high-low)/2;
```

instead of

```cpp
mid = (low + high)/2;
```

Because

```cpp
low + high
```

may cause **Integer Overflow** when the values become very large.

The safer formula is

```cpp
mid = low + (high-low)/2;
```

This is the preferred approach in coding interviews and production code.

---

# 8️⃣ Advantages

✅ Faster than Linear Search

✅ Works in O(log n)

✅ Efficient for large datasets

✅ Reduces search space by half after every comparison

---

# 9️⃣ Limitations

❌ Array must be sorted

❌ Not suitable for linked lists

❌ Sorting may take additional time if data is initially unsorted

