# 📈 Binary Search vs Linear Search

| Feature | Linear Search | Binary Search |
|----------|--------------|---------------|
| Data Requirement | Sorted or Unsorted | Sorted Only |
| Best Case | O(1) | O(1) |
| Average Case | O(n) | O(log n) |
| Worst Case | O(n) | O(log n) |
| Technique | Sequential Search | Divide & Conquer |

---

# 💡 Interview Tips

✅ Binary Search works **only on sorted data**.

✅ Always use

```cpp
mid = low + (high-low)/2;
```

to avoid integer overflow.

✅ Iterative Binary Search is preferred because it uses **constant space**.

✅ Recursive Binary Search is easier to understand but uses additional stack memory.

---

# 📝 Summary

- Binary Search is an efficient searching algorithm.
- It repeatedly divides the search space into two halves.
- The array must be sorted.
- Iterative implementation uses O(1) space.
- Binary Search reduces the search space after every comparison, making it much faster than Linear Search.

